# What is GreasyPidgin?

Greasy Pidgin is an implementation of various algorithmic composition principles. The name refers to the groundbreaking algorithmic toolkit by Michael Edwards 'slippery chicken'. The wordplay on Pigeon - Pidgin refers to 'pidgin languages' - languages that come into existence when people that do not share a common native language come together and form a new language based on their contact language. As I am galumphing through a myriad of programming languages over the years I found little gems - and obstacles - in each environment which led me to this project: Trying to implement everything I love from each environment and unify it in a system that is tailored to my creative and compositional needs.

# Getting started

## Install python

Go to https://www.python.org/downloads and install the latest version of conda according to the given instructions

## Optional: Installing miniconda

Why? Sometimes Python can behave unpredictable and different projects (especially older ones, that are not updated any more) might require different version of python. By using a local environment you can ensure that GreasyPidgin will always work – and all your other projects as well.

Go to https://www.anaconda.com/docs/getting-started/miniconda/install/overview and install miniconda

open a terminal (windows: power shell) and create a new environment in Python 3.14 with the name *greasypidgin*

```{code-cell} bash
conda create --name greasypidgin python=3.14
```

then run

```{code-cell} bash
conda activate greasypidgin
```
to activate it.

## Installing GreasyPidgin
1. Download this repository: click **Code -> Download ZIP**, unzip it where you want to keep it, e.g. university or music theory folder
2. Open a terminal and go to the directory where you unzipped the repository using

```{code-cell} bash
cd path/to/directory/of/GreasyPidgin
```

3. To install GreasyPidgin run:

```{code-cell} bash
pip install -e .
```

4. To test if the installation worked, open Python in the Terminal with

```{code-cell} bash
python3
```

or

```{code-cell} bash
python
```

Then run in the Python shell:


```{code-cell} python
from GreasyPidgin.GreasyPidgin import GreasyPidgin
print(GreasyPidgin())
```

The result should be:

